============================================================

Do not immediately redesign or rewrite the repository.

FIRST audit the existing system.

Preserve:
- canonical data;
- existing identifiers;
- existing provenance;
- previous migration work;
- existing valid schemas;
- existing useful book functionality;
- source history;
- unconventional/historical material;
- author hypotheses;
- negative results;
- archived references.

Never fabricate scientific data to make a record look complete.

Use explicit values such as UNKNOWN, UNAVAILABLE, NOT_MEASURED,
NOT_APPLICABLE, PLANNED, REQUIRES_REVIEW, or equivalent existing
MAT null semantics when evidence is unavailable.

Create a working branch before major changes.

Do not delete legacy/reference material merely because it does not
represent accepted mainstream science.

============================================================

============================================================

Use 0000 and the best-developed early element records as the reference
architecture.

Audit at least:

0000 Origin State
0001 Hydrogen
0002 Helium
0003 Lithium
0004 Beryllium
0005 Boron
0006 Carbon
0007 Nitrogen
0008 Oxygen
0009 Fluorine

Then inspect all subsequent records currently present in the repo,
including Neon, Sodium, Magnesium and later generated/baseline records.

DO NOT assume the point where completeness changes.

Measure it.

Generate a machine-produced RECORD COVERAGE MATRIX.

For every element, compare:

- main manuscript sections;
- canonical data fields;
- data subdocuments;
- sources;
- calculations;
- relationships;
- experiments;
- graphs;
- tables;
- images;
- diagrams;
- models;
- validation state;
- source coverage;
- uncertainty coverage;
- condition coverage;
- domain coverage;
- narrative/history coverage;
- citation coverage;
- cross-link coverage;
- visual coverage;
- review coverage.

Classify each field/content family as something equivalent to:

COMPLETE
PARTIAL
BASELINE
PLACEHOLDER
MISSING
UNKNOWN
NOT_APPLICABLE
REQUIRES_REVIEW

Do not populate missing measurements by guessing.

Save this audit as a generated QA artifact, not as hand-maintained
scientific truth.

============================================================

============================================================

MAT currently has strong factual/data architecture but should read more
like a major reference book.

Add an editorial/narrative layer where appropriate:

- discovery;
- pre-discovery knowledge;
- naming;
- etymology;
- important experiments;
- scientists/researchers;
- major corrections to earlier understanding;
- technological milestones;
- industrial adoption;
- important controversies;
- historical models;
- present scientific understanding;
- remaining open questions.

Do not turn every record into a novel.

Use concise, well-sourced narrative sections that explain WHY the data
matters and HOW human knowledge developed.

History must distinguish:
- contemporary evidence;
- historical belief;
- superseded model;
- disputed interpretation;
- folklore/esoteric interpretation;
- modern verified knowledge.

============================================================

============================================================

MAT intentionally contains more than conventional textbook material.

It may contain:

- established science;
- evaluated reference data;
- derived values;
- computational predictions;
- emerging research;
- hypotheses;
- historical scientific models;
- unconventional research;
- esoteric systems;
- historical claims;
- patents;
- testimony;
- declassified/redacted documents;
- archived claims;
- mysteries;
- disputed claims;
- author concepts;
- negative results;
- alleged experiments;
- claims whose original source cannot currently be verified.

DO NOT DELETE these solely for being unconventional.

DO NOT PRESENT them all as equally true.

Create a strong typed EVIDENCE / CLAIM system.

A claim and its evidence status are separate objects.

Suggested concepts — adapt to current MAT schema rather than blindly
replacing it:

ESTABLISHED
EVALUATED_REFERENCE
REPLICATED_EXPERIMENT
MEASURED
DERIVED
COMPUTATIONAL_PREDICTION
EMERGING
HYPOTHESIS
HISTORICAL_MODEL
SUPERSEDED
UNCONVENTIONAL
ESOTERIC
ANECDOTAL
PATENT_CLAIM
ARCHIVAL_CLAIM
DISPUTED
CONTRADICTED
UNVERIFIED
SOURCE_UNAVAILABLE
REDACTED
AUTHOR_HYPOTHESIS

Also retain:
- confidence;
- provenance;
- date;
- reviewer;
- independent replication;
- source quality;
- counter-evidence;
- known limitations.

Never let an evidence label become a political or editorial censorship
mechanism.

It is metadata so users and AI can tell WHAT KIND OF CLAIM they are
looking at.

============================================================

============================================================

Support multiple reading depths.

Do not create contradictory child versions of facts for:
- primary school;
- secondary school;
- college;
- university;
- postgraduate;
- professional.

Use progressive disclosure / reading modes.

Possible levels:

QUICK
FOUNDATIONAL
ACADEMIC
PROFESSIONAL
RESEARCH
DATA

The canonical science remains the same.

A simpler level changes:
- wording;
- explanation depth;
- terminology support;
- number of visible fields;
- equation depth.

It does NOT change the underlying factual value.

Add glossary/tooltips for terminology.

============================================================

============================================================

Benchmark MAT against the useful characteristics of:

- premium printed reference books;
- scientific handbooks;
- university textbooks;
- dictionaries;
- thesauri;
- encyclopedias;
- Bibles/large cross-referenced works;
- historical codices;
- atlases;
- technical data books;
- field manuals;
- scientific PDFs;
- reports;
- infographics;
- pamphlets/quick-reference books;
- Kindle;
- Apple Books;
- modern EPUB readers;
- Wikipedia;
- NIST-style technical references;
- PubChem-style data references.

Do not imitate one product visually.

Take the strongest interaction pattern for MAT.

The reading UI should feel like a BOOK first and a scientific
workstation when deeper tools are requested.

============================================================

============================================================

Default navigation should be calm.

Large menu trees should start collapsed.

The user previously requested that front matter / foundation / data /
methodology / visualization / index / governance / migration / back
matter be logically grouped rather than permanently dominating the
element navigation.

Use a book/library icon and recognizable publication metaphors where
appropriate.

Provide:

- table of contents;
- chapter/section tree;
- breadcrumbs;
- previous/next;
- related topic links;
- element-to-element links;
- domain navigation;
- index;
- glossary;
- people index;
- source index;
- figure index;
- table index;
- equation index;
- concept index.

Allow deep linking directly to a section/fact/figure where technically
possible.

============================================================

============================================================

Implement OPTIONAL reading pagination.

Do not store canonical content according to visual page numbers.

Web/reflow:
content adapts naturally.

Paginated reader mode:
divide presentation into stable visual page surfaces based on viewport,
typography and content-flow rules.

Print:
generate physical page numbering.

EPUB:
let the reading system reflow according to EPUB conventions.

Provide:
- page progress;
- chapter progress;
- book progress;
- next/previous page;
- keyboard navigation;
- touch/swipe where appropriate;
- restore last reading position.

Avoid breaking:
- tables;
- equations;
- figures;
- captions;
- citation blocks
across pages in unreadable ways.

============================================================

============================================================

Improve:

- measure/line length;
- margins;
- vertical rhythm;
- heading hierarchy;
- paragraph spacing;
- figure placement;
- caption style;
- table readability;
- equation spacing;
- footnotes;
- pullouts/callouts;
- chapter-opening treatment.

Avoid turning every piece of information into a floating dashboard card.

Use cards when information is modular.

Use normal editorial flow when information is narrative.

Allow visual quiet.

A five-star technical book needs rhythm, not maximum density on every
screen.

============================================================

============================================================

Separate these categories:

A. DATA-GENERATED VISUALS
These should be generated deterministically from MAT data where
possible:

- plots;
- line graphs;
- bar charts;
- scatter plots;
- radar plots if scientifically justified;
- phase diagrams where source data permits;
- spectra;
- isotope abundance charts;
- property comparisons;
- relationship diagrams;
- timelines;
- tables.

Do not generate these with image AI when exact scientific values exist.

Generate accessible SVG/Canvas/HTML and data tables behind them.

B. SCIENTIFIC DIAGRAMS
Generate programmatically where possible:
- electron configurations;
- orbital diagrams;
- lattice diagrams;
- process flows;
- relationship maps;
- atom/isotope diagrams;
- energy-level diagrams.

C. ILLUSTRATIVE/ARTISTIC ASSETS
Use an image-generation workflow only where a genuinely illustrative
image is required.

============================================================

============================================================

Inspect all Walter Russell and alternative periodic-arrangement
references currently in downloadfiles and elsewhere.

The purpose is NOT to make Russell's model indistinguishable from the
accepted periodic table.

Create an "Alternative / Historical Models" lane.

For each visual/model distinguish:

- attribution;
- original historical source;
- date;
- claimed meaning;
- MAT reconstruction;
- conventional scientific comparison;
- evidence status;
- criticisms/limitations;
- source links.

The user wants original MAT visual reconstructions including variants
such as:

- conventional table;
- Russell-associated arrangements;
- spiral/radial arrangements;
- musical-note/frequency mappings;
- angular arrangements;
- other relevant historical representations.

Generate ORIGINAL MAT diagrams based on documented geometry/data.

Do not copy reference images pixel-for-pixel.

Where a musical/frequency mapping is not established physical science,
label it according to its actual evidence/model status.

============================================================

============================================================

Preserve useful existing features and improve them only when necessary.

Review support for:

- bookmark;
- reading position;
- notes;
- favorites if distinct from bookmarks;
- citation copying/export;
- search;
- text size;
- theme;
- read aloud;
- print/PDF;
- EPUB;
- translation workflow;
- share/deep-link;
- glossary;
- figure enlargement;
- table expansion;
- copy value + unit + citation.

Translation must never silently alter canonical scientific meaning.

============================================================

============================================================

Desktop:
hover can preview:

- element;
- person;
- definition;
- citation;
- source;
- image;
- equation variable;
- related material.

Mobile/tablet:
the same interaction must work by tap/focus, not depend on hover.

Large images may open in an accessible lightbox/detail viewer with:
- zoom;
- pan;
- caption;
- source;
- alt description;
- download/export only when rights allow.

============================================================

============================================================

Prepare record/section review metadata.

A whole element should not simply be called "verified" because one
chemist checked one table.

Support review scopes such as:

- chemistry;
- physics;
- biology;
- materials;
- medical/pharma;
- mathematics;
- data/provenance;
- historical;
- editorial;
- accessibility.

Possible metadata:

review_status
review_scope
reviewer
credentials/organization where publishable
date
version reviewed
issues
resolution
next_review

Use sensible privacy and contributor rules.

============================================================

============================================================

Provide a controlled "suggest correction/addition" pathway.

A correction must not silently overwrite canonical data.

It should produce a reviewable change/proposal containing:

- record;
- exact field/section;
- existing value;
- suggested value;
- evidence/source;
- rationale;
- submitter metadata if supplied;
- review state.

Architecture should be compatible with Git/GitHub review workflows.

============================================================

============================================================

Treat accessibility as a release requirement.

Target WCAG 2.2 AA for the web reader and applicable EPUB
accessibility practices.

Check:

- semantic landmarks;
- headings;
- keyboard access;
- focus;
- screen reader labels;
- table headers;
- alt text;
- color contrast;
- non-color evidence indicators;
- reduced motion;
- zoom;
- reflow;
- touch targets;
- captions/transcripts;
- equation accessibility;
- citation accessibility;
- print readability.

Animations must respect prefers-reduced-motion.

============================================================

============================================================

MAT is intended to remain useful offline.

Preserve or improve offline capability.

Do not ship all 118+ future records, all high-resolution images and
all 3D assets into the initial browser payload unnecessarily.

Use sensible:
- lazy loading;
- caching;
- indexed/offline metadata;
- generated indexes;
- asset sizing;
- code splitting.

Keep search useful offline where feasible.

============================================================

============================================================

Every graph must have:

- title;
- variable names;
- units;
- axes;
- conditions;
- source;
- uncertainty/error bars where available;
- accessible alternative;
- underlying table/download where suitable.

Never create decorative scientific graphs from invented numbers.

Pie charts should only be used when part-to-whole representation is
actually appropriate.

Do not use radar/spider plots merely for visual novelty.

============================================================

============================================================

Medical, pharmaceutical, toxicity and exposure information must clearly
separate:

- reference data;
- regulatory thresholds;
- established clinical use;
- preclinical research;
- historical claims;
- speculative claims.

Preserve source jurisdiction and date.

Do not let MAT presentation transform experimental material into
medical advice.

============================================================

============================================================

A patent proves that a document/claim was filed or granted according to
its legal record; it does not by itself establish physical efficacy.

Represent accordingly.

For alleged non-public or unavailable material:

do not invent contents.

Record:
- that a reference/claim is reported;
- who reported it;
- where;
- access state;
- what is independently verifiable;
- what remains unavailable.

============================================================

============================================================

Generate professional indexes where practical:

- master index;
- element/material index;
- subject index;
- people index;
- compound index;
- process index;
- application index;
- equation index;
- figure index;
- table index;
- source/bibliography index;
- patent index;
- model/theory index.

For the web these can be interactive.
For print/EPUB derive appropriate equivalents.

============================================================

============================================================

Run both ebookcraft prototypes.

Create a comparative UX matrix covering:

- book feeling;
- typography;
- navigation;
- chapter presentation;
- information density;
- imagery;
- icons;
- interaction;
- page rhythm;
- responsive behavior;
- history/narrative;
- reference usability;
- visual hierarchy;
- accessibility;
- search;
- pagination;
- scientific suitability.

Do NOT simply replace MAT with ebookcraft.

Extract only the better interaction/design ideas.

MAT's scientific architecture remains authoritative.

============================================================

============================================================

After the audit, implement in safe dependency order.

Suggested sequence:

1. protect canonical architecture;
2. establish audit/baseline;
3. fix authority/duplication problems;
4. strengthen typed entity/source/relationship model;
5. introduce domain registry/lenses;
6. improve citation granularity;
7. improve element overview + narrative structure;
8. improve public reader information architecture;
9. implement book-quality reading/pagination behavior;
10. integrate improved visual patterns from ebookcraft;
11. generated visual/data pipeline;
12. accessibility/responsive polish;
13. tests;
14. documentation.

Adapt this order if repository dependencies require it.

============================================================

============================================================

Add/extend automated tests for:

- schema validity;
- ID uniqueness;
- source resolution;
- relationship validity;
- dead internal links;
- external-link reporting;
- generated publication synchronization;
- domain registry;
- evidence states;
- invalid scientific null semantics;
- citations;
- accessibility basics;
- keyboard navigation;
- responsive reader;
- search;
- bookmarks;
- pagination;
- deep linking;
- EPUB generation;
- exports;
- print layouts.

Use Playwright for user journeys.

============================================================

============================================================

Capture after screenshots using the same viewports as before.

Compare:

BEFORE
AFTER

Especially:

- landing/front page;
- MAT:0000;
- Hydrogen overview;
- Hydrogen specialist/domain view;
- history/narrative page;
- data-heavy page;
- graph page;
- source/citation page;
- alternative model page;
- search;
- mobile;
- tablet;
- paperback/A4 preview.

============================================================

============================================================

After implementation update documentation explaining:

- canonical architecture;
- domain lenses;
- evidence lanes;
- knowledge graph;
- citations;
- source registry;
- review workflow;
- publication pipeline;
- generated content;
- public reader vs Studio;
- how to add a new domain;
- how to add a source;
- how to add a relation;
- how to add a visualization;
- how to mark unknown data;
- how to run validation;
- how to build and preview.

Do not overwrite important historical documentation.

============================================================

============================================================

STOP and report rather than fabricate if:

- a canonical schema decision is genuinely ambiguous;
- a migration would destroy scientific provenance;
- a source cannot be verified;
- a number cannot be sourced;
- a licence/rights decision belongs to the repository owner;
- a change would invalidate MAT IDs;
- a generated visualization requires unavailable data.

Otherwise proceed autonomously through the audit, implementation,
testing and visual validation.

============================================================
FINAL PRODUCT STANDARD
============================================================

MAT should feel:

to a casual reader:
like a beautifully produced scientific atlas/book;

to a student:
like a navigable textbook and encyclopedia;

to a scientist:
like a traceable professional reference;

to an engineer:
like a usable technical handbook;

to a historian:
like a sourced history of ideas and discoveries;

to an unconventional/esoteric researcher:
like a comprehensive but evidence-labelled archive;

to an AI:
like a typed, versioned, provenance-rich scientific knowledge graph;

to a developer:
like a stable data platform with deterministic publication outputs.

The target is not "a prettier periodic table".

The target is a five-star, research-grade,
human-and-machine Materials Atlas Table Codex.

Begin by auditing and running the existing repository.
Do not redesign from assumptions.
```

One thing I would **not** ask Codex to do in that first pass is mass-fill all later elements. First make the architecture capable of representing them correctly, run the early-record coverage comparison, and identify the precise point and categories where the production depth drops. Then we can use that report to build the second production prompt that fills Neon onward using the fully locked MAT template without contaminating the records with guessed values.

That separation matters: **architecture first, scientific completion second, image generation third.** The charts, tables, spectra and property plots that are derivable from actual numerical data should be generated by code; only genuine illustrations and original reconstructions need an image-generation pass. That is the safest way to get MAT from a very ambitious repository to something I would consider genuinely publishable and useful as the materials knowledge foundation you have in mind.

The top controls must actually change workflows.

## WRITE

Structure and scientific prose.

Provide:

- headings;
- paragraphs;
- definitions;
- notes;
- citations;
- cross references.

## DESIGN

Publication components:

- text;
- figure;
- image;
- table;
- chart;
- graph;
- equation;
- callout;
- source card;
- timeline;
- diagram;
- 3D placeholder;
- video;
- audio.

## DATA

Display actual structured MAT properties.

Do not edit a JSON-looking fake copy.

## REVIEW

Display:

- scientific errors;
- missing sources;
- incomplete assets;
- accessibility;
- unresolved relationships;
- baseline/curated status.

## PREVIEW

Real preview modes:

```text
Web
EPUB
Tablet
6×9
A4
```

## EXPORT

Actual build/export status.

Do not make these visual-only tabs.

---

Every 118-element interaction must support:

- Tab;
- Enter;
- Space;
- arrow navigation where sensible;
- accessible element name;
- atomic number;
- symbol;
- status.

Example accessible label:

```text
Neon, symbol Ne, atomic number 10, baseline MAT record
```

Do not use colour alone for status.

---

Example:

```bash
npm run validate:elements
```

Check:

- identity;
- schema;
- status;
- provenance;
- conditions;
- invalid null semantics;
- synthetic-element misuse.

Make it part of:

```bash
npm run validate
```

---

Also implement or scaffold where appropriate:

- undo/redo for Studio layout/draft changes;
- breadcrumb;
- element quick search;
- keyboard shortcuts;
- autosave of local Studio UI state;
- command palette;
- component insert menu;
- figure manager;
- table manager;
- source inspector;
- glossary inspector;
- relationship explorer;
- publication readiness;
- missing-assets list;
- accessibility report;
- responsive preview;
- export history;
- build log viewer;
- dirty-file indicator;
- baseline/full-record comparison;
- source/provenance diff.

Do not allow scope creep to prevent the core application from working.

---
